  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nRain chances return Monday and remain into early next week, but\nvery low confidence on whether or not there will be notable \ndrought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure over the Great Lakes moves east, bringing a \nfrontal system that stalls over the northeast Monday into Monday\nnight. Small shortwave on Monday results a chance for light \nshowers in the north beginning Monday evening, continuing into \nTuesday. The question will be how far south the warm front will\npush the rain showers to. As of this update, ensembles keep the\nmajority of the rain north of the Bangor Region. Very minimal \naccumulation with these showers. \n\nTuesday-Wednesday a cutoff low slowly exits northeast into the \nMaritimes allowing for deep layered ridging to build into the \nregion. At the surface, a back door cold front sinks southwest \ninto southwestern Maine Tuesday and Tuesday night before lifting\nback northeast as a warm front Wednesday. Low level \nfrontogenetic forcing to the north/east of this boundary will \nallow for a widespread stratiform rain mainly north of a Bangor-\nMachias line, with moderate rainfall possible for areas mainly \nalong and north of a Greenville-Millinocket-Danforth line. From \nMOnday-Wednesday a quarter to a half of an inch of rain is \npossible along and north of a Mount Katahdin to Amity line, with\nlessor amounts further south, ranging down to at most a few \nhundreths of an inch across Mount Desert Island.\n\nThe region should see a lull in precipitation Wednesday night\nand Thursday morning with the region under the axis of the\naforementioned deep layered ridge, before there is another \nchance of rainfall Thursday afternoon, depending on the timing \nof a slow moving system for late this week/early next week.\n\nThe models remain in disagreement on the timing and track of a\ncutoff low that tracks from the Great Lakes some time \nWednesday-Thursday as it slowly lifts northeast, with another \nclosed low developing to the west Friday/Friday night then \nlifting into the Maritimes in the Sunday-Monday timeframe. This \nsecond closed low could also potentially interact with another \nclosed low off the southeastern then mid-Atlantic seaboard \nadding even more moisture and energy to the system.\n\nThere is the potential that this storm system could bring a\nprolonged period of moderate to heavy rainfall to the region\nfrom late this week into early next week. There is also the\npotential for at least coastal areas to see strong gusty winds\nwith this system as well, with some signs that this could evolve\ninto a southeaster at some point. However, the certainty in \nthis happening is relatively low at this time - as one of the \nthings models do the least well is handle the evolution of \nclosed lows \n- and the forecast from late Thursday-Sunday is dependent on 2 \nto 3 of these features. \n\nGiven this uncertainty, it is to soon to specify what if any\nimpacts could occur from these multiple closed lows late this\nweek and early next week.",
